\lispPackageChapter{sigma/time-series}

The \texttt{sigma/time-series} package supports simple time series and
multivariate time series (time multiseries), together with geometric helpers
such as raster lines through grids, norms, and distances.

A \emph{time series} is a nonempty list whose elements share a common type.  A
\emph{time multiseries} is a nonempty list of arrays of equal dimensions, where
each array holds the data for one time step.

\section{Macros}

\lispMacroSubsection{snap-index}

The \texttt{snap-index} macro wraps an index into the half-open interval
$[0,\textit{bound})$ by adding or subtracting \texttt{bound} as needed.  It
modifies the place \texttt{index} in place.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(snap-index \lispVar{index} \lispVar{bound})}

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{array-raster-line}

The \texttt{array-raster-line} function returns a one-dimensional list of values
from an array along the straight-line path from \texttt{start-point} to
\texttt{end-point}.  The array may be of any rank.  Points are generated by
\texttt{raster-line} and read with \texttt{array-values}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(array-raster-line \lispVar{array} \lispVar{start-point}
  \lispVar{end-point} \&key \lispVar{coordinate-assertion} \lispVar{from-start}
  \lispVar{from-end})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{distance}

The \texttt{distance} function calculates the distance between two points as
the norm of their coordinate-wise difference.  The optional power is as for
\texttt{norm}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(distance \lispVar{initial-point} \lispVar{final-point} \&optional
  \lispVar{power})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(distance '(0 0) '(3 4)) ; Returns 5.0
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{norm}

The \texttt{norm} function returns the mathematical vector norm of a sequence.
For the infinity norm, pass \texttt{:infinity} as the power.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(norm \lispVar{sequence} \&optional \lispVar{power})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{sequence}] A sequence of numbers.
\item[\lispVar{power}] A positive number, or \texttt{:infinity}.  The default
  is $2$ (the Euclidean norm).
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(norm '(3 4))              ; Returns 5.0
(norm '(3 4) :infinity)    ; Returns 4
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{raster-line}

The \texttt{raster-line} function returns the discrete points on a straight
line from \texttt{start-point} to \texttt{end-point} in an $n$-dimensional
integer grid.  It is derived from the three-dimensional scan-conversion
algorithm of Kaufman and Shimony, generalized to any nonnegative dimension.
Optional \texttt{from-start} and \texttt{from-end} trim points from either end
of the basic path.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(raster-line \lispVar{start-point} \lispVar{end-point} \&key
  \lispVar{coordinate-assertion} \lispVar{from-start} \lispVar{from-end})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{start-point}] A list of coordinates.
\item[\lispVar{end-point}] A list of coordinates of the same length.
\item[\lispVar{coordinate-assertion}] A predicate applied to each coordinate
  (default \texttt{integerp}).
\item[\lispVar{from-start}] Nonnegative integer offset from the start.
\item[\lispVar{from-end}] Integer offset from the end.
\end{description}

\lispFunctionSubsection{similar-points?}

The \texttt{similar-points?} predicate returns true if two points are lists of
equal length whose coordinates all satisfy an optional assertion (default
\texttt{numberp}).

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(similar-points? \lispVar{p} \lispVar{q} \&optional
  \lispVar{coordinate-assertion})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{time-series?}

The \texttt{time-series?} predicate returns true if its argument could be a time
series: a nonempty list whose every element is of the given element type
(default \texttt{t}).

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(time-series? \lispVar{time-series} \&optional \lispVar{element-type})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(time-series? '(1.0 2.0 3.0) 'float) ; Returns T
(time-series? '())                   ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{time-multiseries?}

The \texttt{time-multiseries?} predicate returns true if its argument is a time
multiseries: a nonempty list of arrays of equal dimensions.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(time-multiseries? \lispVar{time-multiseries})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{tmsref}

The \texttt{tmsref} function works like \texttt{aref}, but for a time series or
multiseries.  For a one-dimensional time series (a list), it returns the element
at the given time.  For a multiseries, it returns the array element at
\texttt{position} within the array for that time step.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(tmsref \lispVar{time-multiseries} \lispVar{time} \&rest
  \lispVar{position})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(tmsref '(10 20 30) 1) ; Returns 20
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{tms-dimensions}

The \texttt{tms-dimensions} function works like \texttt{array-dimensions}, but
for a time series or multiseries.  The first dimension listed is the time
dimension.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(tms-dimensions \lispVar{time-multiseries})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{tms-values}

The \texttt{tms-values} function returns a list of the values in a time series
or multiseries at the specified positions.  Each position is a list whose first
element is the time index; further elements are spatial subscripts for a
multiseries.  A bare integer is treated as a one-element position list.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(tms-values \lispVar{time-multiseries} \lispVar{positions})}

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{time-multiseries}

The \texttt{time-multiseries} type comprises objects that satisfy
\texttt{time-multiseries?}.
